1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,267
Clean energy deserves clear evidence.

2
00:00:02,267 --> 00:00:09,520
When two plausible solar reports cover the same meter and hour, a spreadsheet can count both.

3
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:14,507
ProofGrid catches that conflict before the records enter renewable energy accounting.

4
00:00:14,507 --> 00:00:24,027
Built for small solar operators and community energy reviewers, it connects data quality, duplicate protection, and verifiable evidence in one workflow.

5
00:00:24,027 --> 00:00:29,468
This is our entry for the Renewable Energy and Energy Trading track.

6
00:00:29,468 --> 00:00:36,426
Start with a meter identifier, a supplied capacity bound, and a simple hourly CSV.

7
00:00:36,426 --> 00:00:42,390
Every row represents one UTC hour and its energy in kilowatt hours.

8
00:00:42,390 --> 00:00:49,348
The workspace brings the generation profile, candidate total, raw readings, and quality findings together.

9
00:00:49,348 --> 00:00:55,809
Reviewers can investigate a suspicious value without switching between spreadsheets and technical logs.

10
00:00:55,809 --> 00:01:04,258
Synthetic examples make onboarding easy, while a historical solar reference demonstrates the same workflow with published data.

11
00:01:04,258 --> 00:01:07,809
Watch what happens with a faulty report.

12
00:01:07,809 --> 00:01:13,389
One reading exceeds the supplied capacity, and another repeats an hour.

13
00:01:13,389 --> 00:01:17,955
ProofGrid identifies the exact problems and blocks the batch.

14
00:01:17,955 --> 00:01:22,521
Clear explanations give the reviewer an actionable correction path.

15
00:01:22,521 --> 00:01:27,594
The validation also checks formatting, continuity, and nonnegative energy values.

16
00:01:27,594 --> 00:01:36,218
An interpretable peak comparison highlights readings for additional attention, keeping automated checks transparent and human review focused.

17
00:01:36,218 --> 00:01:42,467
Now load our historical reference from Open Power System Data and CoSSMic.

18
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:49,238
Twenty four nominal hourly values total forty point seven one nine kilowatt hours.

19
00:01:49,238 --> 00:01:58,092
The repository includes the selected cumulative counters, transformation script, attribution, and file hashes, making this example reproducible.

20
00:01:58,092 --> 00:02:10,070
The screen identifies the processed historical source, approximate hourly boundaries, and assumed ten kilowatt capacity, so reviewers can assess the evidence in context.

21
00:02:10,070 --> 00:02:15,526
After reviewing the profile and any warnings, the reviewer confirms the batch.

22
00:02:15,526 --> 00:02:20,527
ProofGrid executes a real transaction on a local Ethereum Virtual Machine.

23
00:02:20,527 --> 00:02:26,892
Our Solidity contract records the evidence digest and reserves the meter hour slots atomically.

24
00:02:26,892 --> 00:02:32,803
The resulting receipt connects the reviewed dataset to a specific transaction and block.

25
00:02:32,803 --> 00:02:35,985
A database can also check local uniqueness.

26
00:02:35,985 --> 00:02:45,078
Here, the contract makes both the registration rule and its receipt inspectable, demonstrating a mechanism for a future shared registry.

27
00:02:45,078 --> 00:02:51,857
Next, we change the supplied readings and try the same twenty four meter hours again.

28
00:02:51,857 --> 00:02:56,828
All twenty four occupied slots are detected, and registration is blocked.

29
00:02:56,828 --> 00:03:03,608
Changing the file does not create fresh capacity to claim those hours in this registry.

30
00:03:03,608 --> 00:03:14,454
The contract also enforces this boundary: our automated test sends an overlapping transaction, confirms that it reverts, and confirms that no partial registration remains.

31
00:03:14,454 --> 00:03:19,106
Export the accepted evidence as a portable JSON package.

32
00:03:19,106 --> 00:03:22,724
The original passes content and transaction verification.

33
00:03:22,724 --> 00:03:26,860
Now alter a reading: the content check fails.

34
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:34,613
ProofGrid combines a recomputed digest with registry identity, on-chain registration, and the successful receipt event.

35
00:03:34,613 --> 00:03:40,299
Reviewers get a clear result and can see which check failed.

36
00:03:40,299 --> 00:03:47,019
A separate command-line verifier also makes the content-integrity check reproducible outside the interface.

37
00:03:47,019 --> 00:03:52,240
ProofGrid turns scattered renewable energy records into an inspectable review trail.

38
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:57,462
Nine automated tests and the complete local workflow demonstrate the core.

39
00:03:57,462 --> 00:04:07,905
The next step is an operator pilot measuring review time and error detection, followed by trusted meter enrollment and durable registry integration.

40
00:04:07,905 --> 00:04:15,026
Built around a session-local EVM, the prototype makes the complete evidence-review workflow easy to reproduce.

41
00:04:15,026 --> 00:04:22,147
Open code, traceable data, and practical checks provide a concrete foundation for that next step.
